    </section>

    <AdZone size="square" />

    <section>
      <h2>Percentages: Sales, Tips, Tax, and Change</h2>
      <p>Percentage problems come up constantly. You are shopping and see "30% off $85." You split a $124 dinner with a 20% tip four ways. Your revenue went from $47,000 to $52,000 and you need the growth rate. These are not math class problems — they cost you money if you get them wrong.</p>
      <p><strong>Worked example:</strong> A jacket costs $340. It is 15% off. To find the discount: multiply 340 × 0.15 = $51 off. Sale price: $340 − $51 = $289. Now add 8% sales tax: 289 × 0.08 = $23.12. Total you hand over: $289 + $23.12 = $312.12. The <a href="/math/percentage-calculator/">percentage calculator</a> handles all three modes — X% of Y, X is what % of Y, and percentage change — so you skip the mental math on every purchase, raise calculation, or commission check.</p>
      <p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Your revenue went from $47,000 to $52,000. Percentage change: (52,000 − 47,000) / 47,000 × 100 = 10.64% growth. The <a href="/math/percentage-change/">percentage change calculator</a> does this for any two numbers and tells you whether it is an increase or decrease. The <a href="/math/discount-calculator/">discount calculator</a> handles percent-off and fixed-amount discounts in one step. Use the <a href="/math/tip-calculator/">tip calculator</a> to split bills and tips across any number of people — accounts for tax and pre-tax tip too.</p>
    </section>

    <AdZone size="square" />

    <section>
      <h2>Grades: Where You Stand and What You Need on the Final</h2>
      <p>Grade anxiety is almost never about how you have done. It is about what you need next. You have an 82% in the class, the final is worth 20% of your grade, and you want at least an 85% to keep your A- average. How hard do you have to study?</p>
      <p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Current grade 82%, worth 80% of the total. Final exam worth 20%. Target final grade 85%. Required final exam score = (85 − 82 × 0.80) / 0.20 = (85 − 65.6) / 0.20 = 19.4 / 0.20 = 97%. You need a 97% on the final. If you cannot hit that, adjust the target. The <a href="/math/grade-calculator/">grade calculator</a> lets you change any variable — current grade, final weight, or target — until you find a realistic combination. The <a href="/math/gpa-calculator/">GPA calculator</a> converts letter grades and percentages into a 4.0 scale with credit hours and weighted courses, so you see the full picture across every class this semester.</p>
    </section>

    <AdZone size="square" />

    <section>
      <h2>Conversions, Time Zones, and Dates</h2>
      <p>You follow a recipe calling for 250 mL but only have cups. A client in Singapore asks for a meeting at 9:00 SGT and you are in New York. A contract says "90 days from February 10" and you need the exact date. These are not complicated math problems, but they are easy to mess up under pressure.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/math/unit-converter/">unit converter</a> covers 8 categories — length, weight, temperature, volume, speed, area, energy, and pressure — so you can convert centimeters to inches, kilograms to pounds, Fahrenheit to Celsius, and more in one page. Need a specific conversion? The <a href="/math/convert/">dedicated conversion pages</a> cover 15 common pairs like inches to centimeters, miles to kilometers, and pounds to kilograms with precision up to 10 decimal places.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/math/time-zone-converter/">time zone converter</a> shows multiple time zones side by side, which is the only practical way to schedule across 3+ regions. The <a href="/math/date-calculator/">date calculator</a> handles the two most common date problems: days between two dates (for deadlines, billing periods, age verification) and adding or subtracting days from a specific date (contract terms, subscription renewals). It also shows working days and calendar months so you do not accidentally land on a weekend.</p>
    </section>

    <AdZone size="square" />

    <section>
      <h2>Quick Measurements and Everyday Calculations</h2>
      <p>Biggest mistakes in home improvement come from guessing instead of measuring. A room that looks like 200 square feet is actually 192 sq ft (16 ft × 12 ft). That mistake means buying an extra box of flooring you cannot return — or worse, coming up short by one row. The <a href="/math/square-footage/">square footage calculator</a> handles rectangles, triangles, circles, L-shapes, and custom polygons with cost estimation so you buy the right amount of material the first time.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/math/age-calculator/">age calculator</a> gives exact age in years, months, and days along with next birthday countdown — useful for verification forms, benefits eligibility, or curiosity. The <a href="/math/scientific-calculator/">scientific calculator</a> handles everything from basic arithmetic through trig, logarithms, and factorials with keyboard support and calculation history, for when you need more than a phone calculator but do not want to open a separate app.</p>
    </section>

    <AdZone size="square" />

    <section>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <h3>How do I calculate the percentage of a number?</h3>
      <p>Multiply the number by the percentage expressed as a decimal. To find 20% of $150: 150 × 0.20 = $30. For reverse problems — "X is what percent of Y" — divide X by Y and multiply by 100. $30 is what percent of $150? 30 / 150 × 100 = 20%. The <a href="/math/percentage-calculator/">percentage calculator</a> handles both modes.</p>

      <h3>How do I convert centimeters to inches?</h3>
      <p>Divide centimeters by 2.54. Example: 50 cm / 2.54 = 19.69 inches. Going the other way: multiply inches by 2.54. Example: 12 in × 2.54 = 30.48 cm. The <a href="/math/unit-converter/">unit converter</a> and <a href="/math/convert/">dedicated conversion pages</a> handle this and dozens of other conversions instantly.</p>

      <h3>How do I calculate what grade I need on the final?</h3>
      <p>Formula: (target grade − current grade × (1 − final weight)) / final weight. The result is what you need on the final expressed as a percentage. If your target is 90%, current is 85%, and the final is worth 25%: (90 − 85 × 0.75) / 0.25 = (90 − 63.75) / 0.25 = 105%. You cannot reach 90% — the final is not weighted high enough. The <a href="/math/grade-calculator/">grade calculator</a> shows you this instantly so you can adjust your target.</p>

      <h3>How do I calculate the number of days between two dates?</h3>
      <p>Count the days from the start date to the end date. The simplest way is to calculate the difference in total days, which accounts for months with different lengths. The <a href="/math/date-calculator/">date calculator</a> does this automatically and also shows working days (excluding weekends) and the date in calendar months — useful for contract deadlines and billing cycles.</p>

      <h3>How do I calculate square footage of a room?</h3>
      <p>For a rectangle, multiply length by width. A room 15 ft by 12 ft = 180 sq ft. For triangles, multiply base by height and divide by 2. For circles, multiply the radius squared by π (3.1416). The <a href="/math/square-footage/">square footage calculator</a> handles all common shapes and includes cost estimation for flooring, paint, or materials.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
